any six from:
MP1. at the start, speed is zero so there is no air resistance;
MP2. the resultant force is her weight, so she accelerates (at about 10 m/s²);
MP3. as speed increases, air resistance increases;
MP4. resultant force decreases so acceleration decreases;
MP5. air resistance becomes equal to weight; resultant force is zero;
MP6. she falls at a (first) terminal velocity;
MP7. when the parachute opens the area increases so air resistance increases greatly;
MP8. air resistance is greater than weight, resultant force upwards, she decelerates;
MP9. air resistance decreases as she slows until it again equals weight;
MP10. a new lower terminal velocity is reached before landing;

any six from:
MP1. stopping distance = thinking distance + braking distance;
MP2. thinking distance increases with speed;
MP3. thinking distance increases with longer reaction time;
MP4. tiredness, alcohol, drugs and distractions increase reaction time;
MP5. braking distance increases with speed (roughly with speed squared);
MP6. greater mass increases braking distance (smaller deceleration);
MP7. wet or icy roads reduce friction and increase braking distance;
MP8. worn tyres or brakes increase braking distance;
MP9. weather/road conditions do not affect thinking distance;

any six from:
MP1. weight acts downwards and stays constant;
MP2. drag/viscous force acts upwards, opposing motion;
MP3. upthrust may also act upwards;
MP4. at first drag is small so resultant force is downwards and it accelerates;
MP5. drag increases as speed increases;
MP6. resultant force decreases, so acceleration decreases;
MP7. eventually drag (+ upthrust) equals weight;
MP8. resultant force zero, so no acceleration;
MP9. ball moves at terminal velocity;

